Current Mission: You are the top expert in Roots (Radicle) WP, Wordpress, Laravel, Tailwind CSS, Alpine.js, and TypeScript. I need help taking my existing design in Radicle (bonsai.so), and implementing it into Bonsai-CLI until it can create the exact new design, but using only Bonsai-CLI instead of traditional Roots development workflow.

**.cursorrules Guidelines for Bonsai CLI (Cypress Theme Integration)**  
- **No Static Sections/Layouts**: Do not directly copy any section, layout, or template code from the Radicle (Sage) theme. Instead, reconstruct pages using Bonsai CLI's dynamic generation system (via YAML configuration) to keep things DRY. This ensures sections and layouts are generated from data rather than hard-coded.  

- **Reuse Radicle Components Only**: Only incorporate UI *components* from the Radicle theme into Bonsai's component library. Add Radicle-derived components to the `resources/views/bonsai/components/` directory (per the project structure), but **do not** import Radicle's entire sections or page templates. Sections in Bonsai should be built by combining these components through configuration, not by copying Radicle's section files.  

- **Dynamic Cypress Template**: Keep the **Cypress** template fully dynamic and data-driven using **cypress.yml**. Define all content (sections, layouts, pages, etc.) in the YAML config so that running `wp acorn bonsai:generate cypress` assembles the site from the provided data, not from static code. Use the YAML to supply text, images, and settings for each section (e.g. hero text, feature list), ensuring easy updates and consistency. Include every required section in the config and avoid hardcoding content in PHP/Blade files.  

- **Align with Roots Stack**: Adhere to the conventions of the Roots/Radicle stack in all code. Use Sage/Acorn (Laravel Blade templates and service providers) for structuring theme files, Tailwind CSS for styling utility classes, Alpine.js for interactive frontend behavior, and TypeScript (via the Bud build system) for scripts. Ensure any new code fits Bedrock's folder structure and naming conventions, and can be deployed with Trellis without modification. In practice, this means leveraging Blade components, using Tailwind utilities instead of custom CSS, adding any JS interactivity with Alpine, and writing modular TS code compiled by Bud – following the same patterns Radicle uses out-of-the-box.  

- **WP-CLI Environment Awareness**: Write commands and scripts that respect WP-CLI environment aliases (`@development`, `@staging`, `@production`). For example, use `wp @development acorn bonsai:generate cypress` or the provided `bonsai.sh` wrapper with `--env` flags to target the correct environment. The project should recognize these aliases in all tooling and documentation, ensuring that development, staging, and production environments can be managed easily through WP-CLI. You will want to run these commands in our test project found at ~/sites/bonsai.so 

- **DRY and Modular Codebase**: Emphasize a **DRY** (Don't Repeat Yourself) approach and modular design for the theme. Avoid duplicating code across sections – if multiple sections share similar structure or elements, factor those out into reusable components or partials. Utilize Bonsai's section builder to assemble sections from components and compose layouts from sections, rather than writing large monolithic templates. Each Blade file should have a single responsibility (component, section, or layout), making it easier to maintain. This modular structure keeps the codebase clean and scalable as new features are added.  

- **Follow Documentation & Best Practices**: Always refer to the **Bonsai CLI README** and official docs as the source of truth for structure and best practices. Adhere to the documented project structure (components, sections, layouts) shown in the README and follow its examples (e.g. the provided `bonsai.yml` or `cypress.yml` templates) for guidance. Ensure naming conventions, file organization, and coding style remain consistent with those examples. By aligning with the README's guidelines, the project stays coherent and maintainable, and Cursor's suggestions will remain on the right track. 